n/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c2c5892-f74c-576d-86e2-c3d98f1ea70e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c2c5892-f74c-576d-86e2-c3d98f1ea70e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2c5892-f74c-576d-86e2-c3d98f1ea70e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0296fe2c-3620-54f1-9ba1-56fc76e2a57d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3177ddd-2ea5-5752-a73b-5a4850d40c00" "answer-a2030c00-81cb-582d-8ab1-8b808a0ea7d7" "answer-0296fe2c-3620-54f1-9ba1-56fc76e2a57d" "answer-7835779c-d98b-5054-95c1-feb0292ef6ee" "answer-962f0311-8a0a-531a-b2a4-eb199b394c72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3177ddd-2ea5-5752-a73b-5a4850d40c00" :answer/id #uuid "f3177ddd-2ea5-5752-a73b-5a4850d40c00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2030c00-81cb-582d-8ab1-8b808a0ea7d7" :answer/id #uuid "a2030c00-81cb-582d-8ab1-8b808a0ea7d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0296fe2c-3620-54f1-9ba1-56fc76e2a57d" :answer/id #uuid "0296fe2c-3620-54f1-9ba1-56fc76e2a57d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7835779c-d98b-5054-95c1-feb0292ef6ee" :answer/id #uuid "7835779c-d98b-5054-95c1-feb0292ef6ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-962f0311-8a0a-531a-b2a4-eb199b394c72" :answer/id #uuid "962f0311-8a0a-531a-b2a4-eb199b394c72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" "question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/id #uuid "776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139644" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" "answer-2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" "answer-90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" "answer-767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" "answer-02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" :answer/id #uuid "ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3+6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/id #uuid "2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" :answer/id #uuid "90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-6+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" :answer/id #uuid "767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-6-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99" :answer/id #uuid "02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3+6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/id #uuid "95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139645" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4-2x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" "answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" "answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" "answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" "answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/id #uuid "3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2+4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/id #uuid "b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2+4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/id #uuid "580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2-4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/id #uuid "e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4+2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/id #uuid "6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" "question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/id #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41401" :question/problem "The height (in feet) and the average number of points scored per game (APG) of four players on a basketball team are shown in the following table. \n\n| Height $(x)$ | **$6$** | **$6.5$** | **$6.5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| APG $(y)$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}?$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}$ will be $8$  points per game." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/id #uuid "5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" "answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" "answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" "answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" "answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/id #uuid "33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7.75$  points per game" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/id #uuid "bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$  points per game" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/id #uuid "0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8.25$  points per game" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/id #uuid "f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8.75$  points per game" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/id #uuid "d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9$  points per game" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/id #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-41739" :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" "answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" "answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" "answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" "answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/id #uuid "0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$44.7$ inches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/id #uuid "2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$45.8$  inches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/id #uuid "e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$46.2$  inches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/id #uuid "54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$46.9$ inches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134029 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078415 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d76f6fd1-8c28-5a6f-b6d6-f8935ad11046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d4bf850-5813-5f60-9b39-8cfc55a16cca"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d76f6fd1-8c28-5a6f-b6d6-f8935ad11046" :question/id #uuid "d76f6fd1-8c28-5a6f-b6d6-f8935ad11046" :question/math-academy-id "q-7002" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)$ defined by $f(x)=2x-3,$ find ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=2x-3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=2y-3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-3 \\\\ x+3 & =2y \\\\ \\frac{x+3}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2df6be3a-0f6d-5096-bf4e-b8bfa2d2b0ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2df6be3a-0f6d-5096-bf4e-b8bfa2d2b0ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "2df6be3a-0f6d-5096-bf4e-b8bfa2d2b0ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22531293-214b-530c-86f7-cdf18b1da2c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a52d3f85-6eb9-50cb-836e-6465ece0de2d" "answer-2061fb20-1146-54d2-ad25-6ee672ffd1da" "answer-22531293-214b-530c-86f7-cdf18b1da2c4" "answer-eb853d16-fce7-507e-b42a-571f1dc7127d" "answer-5c509b94-d744-51e8-a94a-69794cb063c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a52d3f85-6eb9-50cb-836e-6465ece0de2d" :answer/id #uuid "a52d3f85-6eb9-50cb-836e-6465ece0de2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2061fb20-1146-54d2-ad25-6ee672ffd1da" :answer/id #uuid "2061fb20-1146-54d2-ad25-6ee672ffd1da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22531293-214b-530c-86f7-cdf18b1da2c4" :answer/id #uuid "22531293-214b-530c-86f7-cdf18b1da2c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb853d16-fce7-507e-b42a-571f1dc7127d" :answer/id #uuid "eb853d16-fce7-507e-b42a-571f1dc7127d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c509b94-d744-51e8-a94a-69794cb063c7" :answer/id #uuid "5c509b94-d744-51e8-a94a-69794cb063c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d4bf850-5813-5f60-9b39-8cfc55a16cca" :question/id #uuid "3d4bf850-5813-5f60-9b39-8cfc55a16cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-78669" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4x-3q$, where $q$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=4x-3q\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=4y-3q\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4y-3q \\\\ x+3q & =4y \\\\ \\frac{x+3q}{4} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3q}{4}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0a64a770-8a82-5513-98ff-f8e9991e353a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0a64a770-8a82-5513-98ff-f8e9991e353a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a64a770-8a82-5513-98ff-f8e9991e353a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b870b08a-e761-58e7-b8fc-fce8b75ed795" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60911624-cd75-57a8-a5fd-d42b9a750579" "answer-b870b08a-e761-58e7-b8fc-fce8b75ed795" "answer-4978396b-3692-5598-905a-6992fe350695" "answer-99a2a771-d1ec-5f15-ba4f-5d685e13ac64" "answer-10a7cb10-eff4-505d-bf58-6872fc938c0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60911624-cd75-57a8-a5fd-d42b9a750579" :answer/id #uuid "60911624-cd75-57a8-a5fd-d42b9a750579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3x-2q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b870b08a-e761-58e7-b8fc-fce8b75ed795" :answer/id #uuid "b870b08a-e761-58e7-b8fc-fce8b75ed795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3q}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4978396b-3692-5598-905a-6992fe350695" :answer/id #uuid "4978396b-3692-5598-905a-6992fe350695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+4q}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99a2a771-d1ec-5f15-ba4f-5d685e13ac64" :answer/id #uuid "99a2a771-d1ec-5f15-ba4f-5d685e13ac64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-3q}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10a7cb10-eff4-505d-bf58-6872fc938c0a" :answer/id #uuid "10a7cb10-eff4-505d-bf58-6872fc938c0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-4q}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078653 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119874 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-adab6899-9f13-599d-9b9d-313f4a53055c" "question-45127938-c6a2-5a13-8b44-5358b2433862"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119875 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-634c0345-e97a-5738-8ae3-640a8e78ce93" "question-4038f08b-56e1-5740-bb03-adc638c1a6b4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119870 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-231b9e48-9ef0-506b-a5a1-fc73da681086"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-adab6899-9f13-599d-9b9d-313f4a53055c" :question/id #uuid "adab6899-9f13-599d-9b9d-313f4a53055c" :question/math-academy-id "q-91173" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7975514b-1327-5f70-a5a4-a2499ab3c36c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7975514b-1327-5f70-a5a4-a2499ab3c36c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7975514b-1327-5f70-a5a4-a2499ab3c36c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d228a6a-bde9-526c-92b6-f3b9c6d2dd53" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55b5cc00-611f-5c77-a3e4-4fa552b677f1" "answer-5d5a6ba5-deda-5536-8c5f-86d60dc12567" "answer-53538eaf-3ca1-53e7-9333-cb61969424e2" "answer-63aa3b56-bb47-5f40-b3a8-c0a8da00b436" "answer-6d228a6a-bde9-526c-92b6-f3b9c6d2dd53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55b5cc00-611f-5c77-a3e4-4fa552b677f1" :answer/id #uuid "55b5cc00-611f-5c77-a3e4-4fa552b677f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d5a6ba5-deda-5536-8c5f-86d60dc12567" :answer/id #uuid "5d5a6ba5-deda-5536-8c5f-86d60dc12567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53538eaf-3ca1-53e7-9333-cb61969424e2" :answer/id #uuid "53538eaf-3ca1-53e7-9333-cb61969424e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63aa3b56-bb47-5f40-b3a8-c0a8da00b436" :answer/id #uuid "63aa3b56-bb47-5f40-b3a8-c0a8da00b436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & -4 & 14\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d228a6a-bde9-526c-92b6-f3b9c6d2dd53" :answer/id #uuid "6d228a6a-bde9-526c-92b6-f3b9c6d2dd53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-45127938-c6a2-5a13-8b44-5358b2433862" :question/id #uuid "45127938-c6a2-5a13-8b44-5358b2433862" :question/math-academy-id "q-42930" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{33}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69e4982b-2aae-5d00-991d-b6494c4a55db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69e4982b-2aae-5d00-991d-b6494c4a55db" :answer-field/id #uuid "69e4982b-2aae-5d00-991d-b6494c4a55db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-03920cc4-e1f8-50bf-92b8-43b1b2e14c0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75c87c6c-1364-57a5-9015-96559daffe3d" "answer-03920cc4-e1f8-50bf-92b8-43b1b2e14c0e" "answer-240d25dd-ce33-51bd-b4d9-30068b1a023f" "answer-9c4798fb-d6a8-5a46-9f8e-17d00a574faf" "answer-5f583757-004f-544b-ab61-0bdb788421ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c87c6c-1364-57a5-9015-96559daffe3d" :answer/id #uuid "75c87c6c-1364-57a5-9015-96559daffe3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03920cc4-e1f8-50bf-92b8-43b1b2e14c0e" :answer/id #uuid "03920cc4-e1f8-50bf-92b8-43b1b2e14c0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-240d25dd-ce33-51bd-b4d9-30068b1a023f" :answer/id #uuid "240d25dd-ce33-51bd-b4d9-30068b1a023f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c4798fb-d6a8-5a46-9f8e-17d00a574faf" :answer/id #uuid "9c4798fb-d6a8-5a46-9f8e-17d00a574faf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f583757-004f-544b-ab61-0bdb788421ad" :answer/id #uuid "5f583757-004f-544b-ab61-0bdb788421ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -7 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-634c0345-e97a-5738-8ae3-640a8e78ce93" :question/id #uuid "634c0345-e97a-5738-8ae3-640a8e78ce93" :question/math-academy-id "q-132023" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{41}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{41}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4767d77-4ea5-54fd-8af5-d2063ff21fec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4767d77-4ea5-54fd-8af5-d2063ff21fec" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4767d77-4ea5-54fd-8af5-d2063ff21fec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe7aab0a-5fbf-5e2a-a8eb-5cfb03bbb901" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ea1ce55-966d-5f10-abed-3819f8de0d04" "answer-fe7aab0a-5fbf-5e2a-a8eb-5cfb03bbb901" "answer-b93f13a2-e5b1-5e21-8544-b4448537f761" "answer-88557905-c296-5243-b252-30962e7378f3" "answer-8a9b00b3-88c8-55d0-a71f-b06285ff52ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ea1ce55-966d-5f10-abed-3819f8de0d04" :answer/id #uuid "4ea1ce55-966d-5f10-abed-3819f8de0d04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe7aab0a-5fbf-5e2a-a8eb-5cfb03bbb901" :answer/id #uuid "fe7aab0a-5fbf-5e2a-a8eb-5cfb03bbb901" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b93f13a2-e5b1-5e21-8544-b4448537f761" :answer/id #uuid "b93f13a2-e5b1-5e21-8544-b4448537f761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88557905-c296-5243-b252-30962e7378f3" :answer/id #uuid "88557905-c296-5243-b252-30962e7378f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 10 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a9b00b3-88c8-55d0-a71f-b06285ff52ff" :answer/id #uuid "8a9b00b3-88c8-55d0-a71f-b06285ff52ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 13 & 6 & -11\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4038f08b-56e1-5740-bb03-adc638c1a6b4" :question/id #uuid "4038f08b-56e1-5740-bb03-adc638c1a6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-91219" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-1){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29c80b82-1274-56dd-b869-d31fed57239d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29c80b82-1274-56dd-b869-d31fed57239d" :answer-field/id #uuid "29c80b82-1274-56dd-b869-d31fed57239d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b73a51f1-c568-5f92-aff8-42cc56eb157b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-337cbaa8-fc57-5b7c-903e-2bd27c9209af" "answer-b73a51f1-c568-5f92-aff8-42cc56eb157b" "answer-c02e61c6-21c0-5d92-95e2-e73618a38e09" "answer-aa13465f-e041-55e1-9140-2cd2282116de" "answer-158027fe-d400-5403-b113-28a2067773a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-337cbaa8-fc57-5b7c-903e-2bd27c9209af" :answer/id #uuid "337cbaa8-fc57-5b7c-903e-2bd27c9209af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b73a51f1-c568-5f92-aff8-42cc56eb157b" :answer/id #uuid "b73a51f1-c568-5f92-aff8-42cc56eb157b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c02e61c6-21c0-5d92-95e2-e73618a38e09" :answer/id #uuid "c02e61c6-21c0-5d92-95e2-e73618a38e09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa13465f-e041-55e1-9140-2cd2282116de" :answer/id #uuid "aa13465f-e041-55e1-9140-2cd2282116de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-158027fe-d400-5403-b113-28a2067773a8" :answer/id #uuid "158027fe-d400-5403-b113-28a2067773a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-231b9e48-9ef0-506b-a5a1-fc73da681086" :question/id #uuid "231b9e48-9ef0-506b-a5a1-fc73da681086" :question/math-academy-id "q-42916" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(35-(-9)) \\\\  & =-264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3af53959-1d8a-5698-92dc-98fa9d92ef56"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3af53959-1d8a-5698-92dc-98fa9d92ef56" :answer-field/id #uuid "3af53959-1d8a-5698-92dc-98fa9d92ef56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a5b9969-07fa-546a-9da1-aef831577fe6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a5b9969-07fa-546a-9da1-aef831577fe6" "answer-de604167-4d91-5774-b979-150a3cf404e7" "answer-23794494-b7b9-5ad4-ba11-ebd6f0094b99" "answer-46d92d44-866f-550f-b83c-6f430b5c3fe6" "answer-4c68a53f-d7de-5bed-a987-bf6fbaa3cc30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5b9969-07fa-546a-9da1-aef831577fe6" :answer/id #uuid "3a5b9969-07fa-546a-9da1-aef831577fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-264" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de604167-4d91-5774-b979-150a3cf404e7" :answer/id #uuid "de604167-4d91-5774-b979-150a3cf404e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-462" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23794494-b7b9-5ad4-ba11-ebd6f0094b99" :answer/id #uuid "23794494-b7b9-5ad4-ba11-ebd6f0094b99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46d92d44-866f-550f-b83c-6f430b5c3fe6" :answer/id #uuid "46d92d44-866f-550f-b83c-6f430b5c3fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c68a53f-d7de-5bed-a987-bf6fbaa3cc30" :answer/id #uuid "4c68a53f-d7de-5bed-a987-bf6fbaa3cc30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "624" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bff2c616-f045-5186-8916-8acfc055fce7" "question-97e18a22-6fdc-5ccf-b5b0-b0a200f3d5b5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bff2c616-f045-5186-8916-8acfc055fce7" :question/id #uuid "bff2c616-f045-5186-8916-8acfc055fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333304" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfb04862-ac9b-5f21-97c1-b9e7e940122c" "answer-field-df54410e-bc2b-5cd4-bd11-c48ccd15d2ff" "answer-field-1cb0f973-9d38-54ed-bd98-9ab310f803cc" "answer-field-d72da840-5c03-5060-8a83-da30b57b93b9" "answer-field-e242fc05-5a80-55d0-b061-b01a07d2d103" "answer-field-a32a75f5-ef1d-5012-9e7e-8a6d623035dc" "answer-field-668bfdfe-e192-5f21-8b18-3abd6df0cf17" "answer-field-01c6a10f-ebe8-58c0-a15f-3c5474e1e637"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfb04862-ac9b-5f21-97c1-b9e7e940122c" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb04862-ac9b-5f21-97c1-b9e7e940122c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0ad8aa71-7dcc-53a7-80cc-ee9c258321b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a83cbb15-c2b0-51de-9c0d-a91b9bf42153" "answer-0ad8aa71-7dcc-53a7-80cc-ee9c258321b7" "answer-c8e9d164-6cbd-5d65-9cfb-1c7f1bc11422" "answer-dcf81baf-3c3b-5532-b109-a4a6a5ac3b60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a83cbb15-c2b0-51de-9c0d-a91b9bf42153" :answer/id #uuid "a83cbb15-c2b0-51de-9c0d-a91b9bf42153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t{e}^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ad8aa71-7dcc-53a7-80cc-ee9c258321b7" :answer/id #uuid "0ad8aa71-7dcc-53a7-80cc-ee9c258321b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/val